// Pull request author: yana-ivanov
// Change: new rule
// Title: Azazel Ransom Note Creation - Linux
// Sigma ID: dd37c267-b3a1-4903-8dd6-9d95c87ceb48
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Yana Ivanov
// Date: 2026-10-11 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: impact, T1657, T1491.001
// Tags: detection.emerging-threats
// Description: Detects creation of the ransom note used by the Azazel extortion operation, an affiliate of The
//   Gentlemen ransomware that conducted independent extortion against more than two dozen
//   organizations and published victims on its own leak site. CloudSEK documented the note being
//   written to /root and /home/ubuntu, and the same extortion message being placed into system login
//   surfaces including /etc/motd, the SSH banner via sshd_config, the PostgreSQL cluster_name
//   parameter and a pgAdmin template, so that administrators encounter it on login. This rule
//   detects the note filename only. The login surface modifications are not detected here because
//   configuration management tooling rewrites those files legitimately.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely
// References:
//   - https://www.cloudsek.com/blog/caught-in-4k-the-gentlemen-files
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/d9cb86be67c14e594cc207cc2dfc8a6787f5bae6/rules-emerging-threats/2026/TA/Azazel/file_event_lnx_azazel_ransom_note.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceFileEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "ATTENTION_SENSITIVE_INFORMATION.txt"
